[al:新概念英语（三）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:A Happy Discovery]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.38]Lesson 34 | 第34课
[00:02.39]A happy discovery | 幸运的发现
[00:09.99]What was the 'happy discovery' ? | 那“幸运的发现”是什么？
[00:14.53]Antique shops exert a peculiar fascination on a great many people. | 古玩店对许多人有一种特殊的魅力。
[00:21.20]The more expensive kind of antique shop where rare objects are beautifully displayed in glass cases | 高档一点的古玩店为了防尘，把珍奇物品漂亮地陈列在玻璃柜中，
[00:29.11]to keep them free from dust is usually a forbidding place. | 那里通常令人望而却步。
[00:34.65]But no one has to muster up courage to enter a less pretentious antique shop. | 但不摆架子的古玩店，人们就不必鼓起勇气才能进去。
[00:41.64]There is always hope that in its labyrinth of musty, dark, disordered rooms a real rarity will be found amongst the piles of assorted junk that litter the floors. | 人们总希望在那些发霉、阴暗、杂乱无章的迷宫般的房间里，从堆满地板、杂七杂八的破烂货中找到真正的珍品。
[00:55.10]No one discovers a rarity by chance. | 没有人能碰巧发现珍品。
[00:59.29]A truly dedicated bargain hunter must have patience, | 一个真正执着的淘宝者必须有耐心，
[01:03.19]and above all, the ability to recognize the worth of something when he sees it. | 而且最重要的是，必须具备一眼看出物品价值的能力。
[01:09.74]To do this, he must be at least as knowledgeable as the dealer. | 要做到这一点，他至少要和古董商一样懂行。
[01:15.71]Like a scientist bent on making a discovery, | 像一心想有所发现的科学家那样，
[01:19.36]he must cherish the hope that one day he will be amply rewarded. | 他必须心怀希望，相信自己总有一天会得到丰厚的回报。
[01:25.20]My old friend, Frank Halliday, is just such a person. | 我的老朋友弗兰克·哈利戴正是这样一个人。
[01:30.90]He has often described to me how he picked up a masterpiece for a mere ￡50. | 他常向我讲述他如何仅花50英镑就买到一件杰作。
[01:37.51]One Saturday morning, Frank visited an antique shop in my neighbourhood. | 一个星期六的上午，弗兰克来到我家附近的一家古玩店。
[01:44.16]As he had never been there before, he found a great deal to interest him. | 由于他从未来过，很多玩意儿都使他感兴趣。
[01:50.22]The morning passed rapidly and Frank was about to leave when he noticed a large packing case lying on the floor. | 上午很快过去了，弗兰克正准备离去，忽然发现地板上放着一只大货箱。
[01:59.25]The dealer told him that it had just come in, but that he could not be bothered to open it. | 店主告诉他箱子是刚进的货，但他懒得打开。
[02:06.29]Frank begged him to do so and the dealer reluctantly prised it open. | 弗兰克恳求他打开，店主勉强撬开了箱子。
[02:12.14]The contents were disappointing. | 里面的东西令人失望。
[02:14.85]Apart from an interesting-looking carved dagger, the box was full of crockery, much of it broken. | 除了一把样子有趣的雕刻匕首，箱子里全是陶器，而且大部分都碎了。
[02:23.03]Frank gently lifted the crockery out of the box and suddenly noticed a miniature painting at the bottom of the packing case. | 弗兰克轻轻地把陶器拿出箱子，突然发现货箱底部有一幅微型画。
[02:33.16]As its composition and line reminded him of an Italian painting he knew well, he decided to buy it. | 画面构图与线条使他想起一幅他熟悉的意大利绘画，于是他决定把画买下来。
[02:42.44]Glancing at it briefly the dealer told him that it was worth ￡50. | 店主草草看了一眼画，告诉他画值50英镑。
[02:48.21]Frank could hardly conceal his excitement, for he knew that he had made a real discovery. | 弗兰克几乎无法掩饰内心的激动，因为他知道自己发现了一件真品。
[02:55.47]The tiny painting proved to be an unknown masterpiece by Correggio and was worth hundreds and thousands of pounds. | 这幅小小的画后来被证明是柯勒乔的一件未被发现的杰作，价值数十万英镑。
